{"name":"Favourite Fudge","hex":"#877252","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"favourite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Most-liked. (In other words, [person]'s favorite [noun] = the [noun] that [person] likes the most.)","Liked, cherished."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person or thing who enjoys special regard or favour.","A person who is preferred or trusted above all others."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/favourite"},{"term":"fudge","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of very sweet candy or confection, usually made from sugar, butter, and milk or cream.","Chocolate fudge."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To try to avoid giving a direct answer.","To alter something from its true state, as to hide a flaw or uncertainty, deliberately but not necessarily dishonestly or immorally."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fudge"}],"entries":[{"title":"Favourite","blocks":[{"text":"In history and politics, a favourite was the intimate companion of a ruler or other important person. In post-classical and early-modern Europe, among other times and places, the term was used of individuals delegated significant political power by a ruler. It was especially a phenomenon of the 16th and 17th centuries, when government had become too complex for many hereditary rulers with no great interest in or talent for it, and political institutions were still evolving. From 1600 to 1660 there were particular successions of all-powerful minister-favourites in much of Europe, particularly in Spain, England, France and Sweden."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Count-Duke_of_Olivares.jpg/330px-Count-Duke_of_Olivares.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Favourite"},{"title":"Fudge","blocks":[{"text":"Fudge is a confection made by mixing sugar, butter, and milk. Modern fudge has its origins in the 19th century United States, and became popular in American women's colleges late in the century. Fudge can come in a variety of flavorings depending on the region or country it was made; popular flavors include fruit, nut, chocolate, and caramel. Fudge is often bought as a gift from a gift shop in tourist areas and attractions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Handmade_fruit_fudge_squares.jpg/330px-Handmade_fruit_fudge_squares.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fudge"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg/500px-Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg/1280px-Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg/500px-Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Duke of Buckingham by the workshop of Rubens (1617–1628)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APortrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_(by_Peter_Paul_Rubens).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Triple_Portrait_of_Cardinal_de_Richelieu_probably_1642%2C_Philippe_de_Champaigne.jpg/500px-Triple_Portrait_of_Cardinal_de_Richelieu_probably_1642%2C_Philippe_de_Champaigne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia